.\"
.\" GETRLIMIT(2) - Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH GETRLIMIT 2 "2026-08-22" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
getrlimit, setrlimit \- get and set resource limits
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RB ( libc ", " \-l:libc.so.0 )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/resource.h>
.PP
.BI "int getrlimit(int " resource ", struct rlimit *" rlim ");
.BI "int setrlimit(int " resource ", const struct rlimit *" rlim ");
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each process has a soft and a hard limit on the resources it may consume.
.BR getrlimit ()
reads the pair for
.IR resource ,
and
.BR setrlimit ()
replaces it:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
struct rlimit {
    rlim_t rlim_cur;  /* soft limit: the one enforced */
    rlim_t rlim_max;  /* hard limit: ceiling for rlim_cur */
};
.EE
.in
.PP
The soft limit is what the kernel enforces.
The hard limit is the ceiling the soft limit may be raised to.
A process may lower either limit, and may raise the soft limit up to the hard
limit; raising the
.B hard
limit requires an effective UID of 0.
Lowering a hard limit is therefore irreversible for an unprivileged process,
which is what makes it useful for confining a child before
.BR execve (2).
.PP
.B RLIM_INFINITY
means no limit.
.PP
Limits are inherited by
.BR fork (2)
and preserved across
.BR execve (2).
.SS Resources
.TP
.B RLIMIT_CPU
CPU seconds, user plus system.
On reaching the soft limit the process is sent
.B SIGXCPU
once per second; on reaching the hard limit it is sent
.BR SIGKILL ,
which it cannot decline.
.TP
.B RLIMIT_FSIZE
Largest regular file, in bytes, the process may create.
A write starting at or beyond the limit fails with
.B EFBIG
and raises
.BR SIGXFSZ ;
a write that merely crosses the limit is truncated to what fits and succeeds.
.BR ftruncate (2)
may not grow a file past it.
Pipes, sockets and terminals are unaffected.
.TP
.B RLIMIT_DATA
Size of the data segment.
.BR brk (2)
will not move the break past this many bytes above its starting value.
.TP
.B RLIMIT_STACK
Size of the stack.
The kernel will not extend the stack past this limit on a fault, so a process
that exceeds it takes
.B SIGSEGV
rather than growing without bound.
.TP
.B RLIMIT_CORE
Largest core file, in bytes.
A soft limit of 0 disables core dumps entirely; substrate treats any non-zero
value as "dump permitted" and does not truncate.
.TP
.B RLIMIT_MEMLOCK
Bytes of memory that may be locked.
Consulted by
.BR mlock (2)
and, for a process that has called
.BR mlockall (2)
with
.BR MCL_FUTURE ,
by
.BR mmap (2).
.TP
.B RLIMIT_NPROC
Processes the invoking real UID may have in existence.
.BR fork (2)
fails with
.B EAGAIN
at the limit.
A process with an effective UID of 0 is exempt, so a limit set on a user can
never lock the administrator out of the machine.
.TP
.B RLIMIT_NOFILE
One greater than the highest descriptor number the process may open.
.BR open (2)
and friends fail with
.BR EMFILE .
The value is silently clamped to the size of the kernel descriptor table.
.TP
.B RLIMIT_AS
.TQ
.B RLIMIT_VMEM
Size of the process address space.
.BR mmap (2)
fails with
.B ENOMEM
when a mapping would push the total past the limit, which is what makes
.BR malloc (3)
fail rather than consuming all of memory.
.TP
.B RLIMIT_RSS
.TQ
.B RLIMIT_SBSIZE
Stored and reported, but not enforced.
They exist so that software which sets them still builds and runs.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, zero is returned.
On error, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I rlim
points outside the accessible address space.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I resource
is not a valid resource, or the requested
.I rlim_cur
exceeds
.IR rlim_max .
.TP
.B EPERM
An unprivileged process tried to raise its hard limit.
.SH EXAMPLE
Confine a child to 64 descriptors and a 1 MiB file size, then exec it.
Lowering the hard limits means the child cannot undo either:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
struct rlimit rl;

rl.rlim_cur = rl.rlim_max = 64;
if (setrlimit(RLIMIT_NOFILE, &rl) == \-1) {
    perror("setrlimit");
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}

rl.rlim_cur = rl.rlim_max = 1024 * 1024;
if (setrlimit(RLIMIT_FSIZE, &rl) == \-1) {
    perror("setrlimit");
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}

execv(path, argv);
.EE
.in
.SH NOTES
The shell's
.B ulimit
builtin is the interactive front end to these calls; it is a builtin rather
than a program precisely because it must change the shell's own limits so
that children inherit them.
.PP
.BR ulimit (2)
is the older System V interface to the same limits, and remains available.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ulimit (2),
.BR fork (2),
.BR execve (2),
.BR getrusage (2),
.BR mlock (2),
.BR mmap (2)
